ntp_io_linux: fall back to packet if_index when timestamp if_index is 0

The kernel fills SCM_TIMESTAMPING_PKTINFO.if_index by resolving the
skb napi id (put_ts_pktinfo). Drivers whose napi is not attached to
the delivering net_device (ti cpsw uses an unregistered dummy) and
kernels without usable busy-poll ids deliver the cmsg with if_index 0,
which defeats the HW clock lookup and silently degrades every
timestamp to kernel timestamps. Treat 0 as invalid and use the
IP_PKTINFO interface instead.

--- a/ntp_io_linux.c
+++ b/ntp_io_linux.c
@@ -735,7 +735,7 @@ NIO_Linux_ProcessMessage(SCK_Message *message, NTP_Local_Address *local_addr,
   iface = NULL;

   ts_if_index = message->timestamp.if_index;
-  if (ts_if_index == INVALID_IF_INDEX)
+  if (ts_if_index == INVALID_IF_INDEX || ts_if_index == 0)
     ts_if_index = message->if_index;
   l2_length = message->timestamp.l2_length;

